        "scanners": {
          "vt": {
            "status": "clean",
            "verdict": "benign",
            "normalizedStatus": "clean",
            "analysis": "Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: sherpamind\nVersion: 0.1.0\n\nSherpaMind is a well-engineered SherpaDesk integration for OpenClaw that provides data ingestion, analysis, and retrieval capabilities. It uses a local SQLite database for storage and implements a background synchronization service via a systemd user unit (sherpamind.service). The codebase demonstrates high security maturity, utilizing parameterized SQL queries in src/sherpamind/analysis.py to prevent injection and safe subprocess execution in src/sherpamind/service_manager.py. The SKILL.md instructions are focused on operational safety, such as checking host prerequisites (python3, systemctl) before installation, and the tool even includes a simple local vector search implementation in src/sherpamind/vector_index.py to avoid external API dependencies for embeddings.",
            "source": "palm",
            "checkedAt": 1774112811435
          },
          "skillspector": null,
          "llm": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "verdict": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "confidence": "medium",
            "summary": "The bundle appears to implement a full local SherpaDesk backend that reasonably matches its description, but the registry metadata underreports required credentials and the runtime instructions ask to install a user-level service and create local state — these mismatches and privileges warrant caution and review before installation.",
            "dimensions": [
              {
                "detail": "The repo and SKILL.md content align with the claimed purpose: a local-first SherpaDesk ingest/sync/analysis backend plus an OpenClaw skill-front. The included code (ingest, DB, vector/index, CLI, service runtime) is consistent with the stated features. However, the skill registry declares no required environment variables or primary credential even though the code and docs expect SherpaDesk credentials (SHERPADESK_API_KEY, SHERPADESK_ORG_KEY, SHERPADESK_INSTANCE_KEY, etc.). That mismatch is an incoherence between manifest metadata and actual needs.",
                "label": "Purpose & Capability",
                "name": "purpose_capability",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "SKILL.md instructs the agent to run repo-local CLI commands (python3 scripts/run.py ...) and to read generated artifacts under .SherpaMind/public/docs and .SherpaMind/private/config.env. It also describes an install/onboarding flow that writes local config (configure --api-key), seeds data from the SherpaDesk API, and can install/manage a user-level systemd service. The instructions explicitly require reading/writing local workspace paths and consuming API tokens. They do not ask for unrelated system files, but they do give the agent broad discretion to run many CLI commands and to interact with the network (API, pip) as part of bootstrap/setup.",
                "label": "Instruction Scope",
                "name": "instruction_scope",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The registry lists no formal install spec, but the bundle contains scripts/bootstrap.py which creates a venv and runs pip to install requirements from requirements.txt and then installs the package. That is a standard Python bootstrap but it performs network package installs (PyPI) and writes runtime artifacts into a workspace-level .SherpaMind directory. The absence of a declared install mechanism in the registry while including an automated bootstrap script is an operational mismatch the user should notice.",
                "label": "Install Mechanism",
                "name": "install_mechanism",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "Registry requires no env vars, yet README, SKILL.md, and scripts reference and create a config file template containing SHERPADESK_API_KEY, SHERPADESK_API_BASE_URL, SHERPADESK_ORG_KEY, SHERPADESK_INSTANCE_KEY, SHERPAMIND_NOTIFY_CHANNEL, and other runtime controls. The skill needs access to SherpaDesk API credentials to function; that credential requirement is missing from the published metadata. Requesting an API token and storing it in .SherpaMind/private/config.env is reasonable for the skill's purpose, but the metadata omission is misleading and increases risk of accidental credential exposure if users aren't warned.",
                "label": "Credentials",
                "name": "environment_proportionality",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The project is designed to create persistent local state under a workspace-level .SherpaMind/ (private DB, logs, runtime venv, public docs) and can install a user-level systemd service (unit name: 'sherpamind.service'). These are legitimate for a long-lived backend but represent significant system persistence and configuration changes (service unit files, installed venv, pip packages). The skill does not set always:true, but it can be installed to run autonomously as a user service — users should be comfortable with a skill that writes runtime state and can manage a user systemd unit.",
                "label": "Persistence & Privilege",
                "name": "persistence_privilege",
                "rating": "concern"
